.PP
.nf
 @section motivation Motivation
.fi
.PP

.PP
Quadratic triangular finite-element with local interpolation function and Jacobian transformation capability\&. \fBElement\fP numbering:

.PP
.PP
.nf
       / s-axis (of local coordinate system)
   3 /
   o
  / \\
.fi
.PP
 6 o o 5 / \\ o -- o -- o -----> r-axis 1 4 2

.PP
The segment nodes are represented by IntegrationPoint variables\&. The local coordinates of the integration points are stored in the array objects 'ri' and 'si'\&.

.PP
The coordinates of the local coordinate system are chosen such that the nodes have the following local coordinates:

.PP
N1 = 0,0, N2 = 1,0, N3 = 0,1, N4 = 0\&.5,0, N5 = 0\&.5,0\&.5, N6 = 0, 0\&.5
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
Since the quadratic triangle uses local shape functions, the integration procedure is as follows\&.

.PP
The weights of the interpolation functions always must add up to 1\&. Thus, since there are 3 integration points, each weight is 0\&.33\&.\&.\&. 
.SH "Constructor & Destructor Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "csmp::IsoparametricQuadraticTriangle::IsoparametricQuadraticTriangle (uint32_t dimensions)\fR [explicit]\fP"
Default constructor initializes triangle to use local coordinates and a three-point integration scheme where the Gauss points are located at the midside nodes\&. The weights of the integration points are set to 1/3 such that the integrated contributions add up to 1\&. \&. 
.SH "Member Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "double csmp::IsoparametricQuadraticTriangle::AspectRatio ()\fR [virtual]\fP"
Computes the element aspect ratio as the ratio between the longest and the shortest boundary segment\&. This rule differs from that presented for the straight-sided triangle which is is discussed in the \fBLinearTriangle\fP subclass\&.
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
The \fBElement\fP is consulted for its global coordinates\&. 
.PP
Reimplemented from \fBcsmp::FiniteElement\fP\&.
