.SS "void csmp::IsoparametricQuadraticTriangle::ExtrapolateIntegrationPointVariableToNodes (uint32_t nvars, const std::vector< double > & IVAR, std::vector< double > & NVAR) const\fR [virtual]\fP"
Because, in the local coordinate system, the triangle is always straight sided, and the derivatives of the interpolation functions are bilinear, variable values computed at integration points can simply be extrapolated to the nodes using an approach as for the linear triangular element\&. This involves the steps:

.PP
.IP "0." 4
Decide which case is dealt with in terms of the integration points which are used\&.
.IP "1." 4
Compute the interpolation function coefficients for the triangle which is defined by the three integration points\&.
.IP "2." 4
For each node point compute the values of the interpolation functions and use these to extrapolate the values of the variables at the nodes\&. 
.PP

